  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(u)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $3$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
